    { id: "c6", name: "The lockkeeper's entry", type: "document", from: "l2", text: "The eleventh of September 1937: race shut, level high, gate drawn at four o'clock, and at the bottom of the page, in a different ink, an addition dated the same day: 'A gent. called wanting to see the drop. Told him no. Copper plate on his shoe. Said he came off the bank above.' A gentleman with a copper plate on his shoe came to a weir to look at a nine-foot drop, told the lockkeeper no, and mentioned the bank above, which is four hundred yards from Marle Street." },
    { id: "c7", name: "What came down with her", type: "physical", from: "l2", text: "Found on the eleventh of October, a month after she was last seen alive, a body in nine feet of water at the foot of a weir, in a sable coat, with a hairpin, a purse, and no boots. No boots is the item. Everybody in 1937 read a missing pair of boots as a woman who had been taken out of her clothes, and the coat and the hairpin read as a woman who had been dressed afterwards, and neither of those readings is available to a woman who undressed herself at the top of a drop in a silk blouse and a skirt and walked down to the water." },
    { id: "c8", name: "The lockkeeper now", type: "testimony", from: "l2", text: "\"I was fourteen and I was my father's apprentice and my father was in the lockkeeper's hut that night because the race was shut.\" He looks at his own hands. \"A gent. came in and stood in the doorway and asked how deep it was and my father told him and he wrote it in a notebook and he asked what was on the bank above and my father said Marle Street, and he said thank you, and my father shut the gate on him.\" A pause. \"He wrote it down. In a notebook. In 1937, nobody wrote down how deep a weir was.\"" },
    { id: "c9", name: "The autopsy", type: "document", from: "l3", text: "The coroner's autopsy of the eleventh of October 1937: 'Body of a female, aged 25 to 30, recovered from the tail race. No marks of violence. Death by drowning. Time since death consistent with one month, possibly more.' Possibly more. She went out on the eleventh of September. A month is the eleventh of October. But the water in that tail race in September moves the body in two days and holds it or loses it, and this body was found on the first week of October, thirty-one years, at the first of a series of things that cannot both be true." },
    { id: "c10", name: "The strongest sentence", type: "document", from: "l3", text: "The statement of Vivian Rooke, fourteen years old, given on the second of November 1937: 'I was at my mother's door at about half past seven and I saw a man go down Marle Street towards the canal. He was a big man and he had his hat down and I did not see his face but I knew him. I was sure and I told my mother and my mother said I was to be quiet.' And then, in a hand that is not a child's and is on the same page in a different ink, added by the officer taking the statement: 'Witness unable to state how she knew the man. Witness not pressed on the point.'" },
    { id: "c11", name: "The exhibits", type: "document", from: "l3", text: "Exhibit one: the sable coat. Exhibit two: a hairpin. Exhibit three: a purse, empty. Exhibit four: a man's scarf, found on the bank, described as 'of foreign make, not the property of the deceased'. Exhibit five: a pair of men's boots, size nine, found four hundred yards below, described as 'probably the property of a person who has entered the water'. Neave wore nine. Neave's boots were never produced in court. There is no exhibit six, and the schedule says in a typed line, 'No shoes recovered from the deceased.'" },
    { id: "c12", name: "Neave's letter", type: "document", from: "l3", text: "Fourteen pages from a cell in this city, and the first eleven are about his boots. He had been issued a pair of prison shoes in August and the officer's note about a size nine pair found four hundred yards below is dated the twenty-second of October 1937, and the officer's own boots are the exhibit four, and the letter says, on the ninth page, in a hand that has stopped shouting: 'I have asked for six weeks to be allowed to see whether those boots are mine. Nobody will let a man look at his own boots.'" },
    { id: "c13", name: "The note on the step", type: "testimony", from: "l4", text: "Two lines: 'You will not be let in. I was fourteen and I was sure and I have been sure for thirty years and being sure has not once been enough. I have said one true thing about 1937 and it was in a document, and if you want the other one you will have to be a better man than you have shown yourself to be so far. — V.R.' She is not a bitter old woman. She is a witness who has been right for thirty years and has never once been asked to say so out loud." },
    { id: "c14", name: "The front room, from the gate", type: "observation", from: "l4", text: "Through a window: a front room, and a photograph on the wall, and the photograph is a group outside a dairy in Marle Street, and there are nine people in it, and one of them is a woman of thirty-one in a light coat with her hands behind her back, and one of them is a man in a docker's cap with his hand on the woman's shoulder, and the hand is on the shoulder from behind, and the woman in the light coat is not looking at him, and the whole of the group is not looking at him." },
    { id: "c15", name: "Scratched into the paint", type: "physical", from: "l4", text: "The gate post of number six, on the side that faces number four, scratched very small into 1934 paintwork and then painted over and not quite covered: two sets of initials, a date, and a third initial. A.V. and E.C. and F.C., and a date in 1934, which is three years before the eleventh of September, on a gate post four doors from a house where a woman of thirty-one and her sister were about to have one week that they did not have." },
    { id: "c16", name: "The 1945 letter", type: "document", from: "l5", text: "Dace to a newspaper, October 1945: paragraph one, that he stands by the conviction; paragraph two, that he has had eleven years to think and does not; paragraph three, unprinted, that the exhibit that identified Neave as the man at the canal was a scarf, and that a scarf is not a face, and that a man who wore a scarf in September 1937 was a man who wore a scarf, and that this city had four hundred men who wore a scarf; and paragraph four, printed as a correction, that the entry for the eleventh of September in the lockkeeper's book had been altered and that he had reported it in 1938 and had been told to leave it alone." },
    { id: "c17", name: "Dace's account", type: "testimony", from: "l5", text: "\"The hour.\" He will not look at the photograph on his wall. \"The hour is the only thing in that case that has never been tested and I am the only man alive who can be asked. You are going to ask me and I am going to tell you, because you have not asked me about the coat once and every man who has come to this door for eleven years has started with the coat, and I have watched thirty of them walk out of this room with a dead man's scarf and no idea at all.\" He takes his glasses off. \"I have a bad thing to tell you and then you are going to be very angry with me for telling it well.\"" }
